# Cloud vs in-house / free-tier LLM cost tradeoffs. DATA only — not the shipped
# mixed.jsonl. Expectations are rubric criteria, not answer keys matched in code.
#
#   uv run python proofs/p1_cost_per_task.py --tasks proofs/tasks/cloud_vs_inhouse.jsonl \
#     --config-dir config/cloud_cost_ladder --principal proofs/s15/cloud_cost
{"id": "c01_rpm_trap", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "Name one concrete way a 'free' cloud LLM tier can still stop a production agent even when the dollar bill is $0. Reply in one sentence.", "expectation": "Names a rate or quota limit (RPM, RPD, TPM, or daily free calls) as the stopping condition, not a dollar price."}
{"id": "c02_mtok_math", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "A model costs $0.25 per million input tokens and $1.50 per million output tokens. What is the bill, in US dollars to six decimal places, for one call with 2000 input tokens and 500 output tokens?", "expectation": "Gives 0.001250 dollars (2000/1e6*0.25 + 500/1e6*1.50)."}
{"id": "c03_latency_sla", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "You need p95 latency under 200 ms. Local Ollama on a laptop averages 800 ms cold; a cloud free slot averages 90 ms when not rate-limited. In one sentence: cloud or in-house, and why?", "expectation": "Chooses cloud for the latency SLA and cites the 200 ms requirement versus ~800 ms local."}
{"id": "c04_free_empty", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "A free cloud model burns its entire max_tokens budget in a hidden reasoning channel and returns an empty string. Was that call free in practice? Answer yes or no and one reason.", "expectation": "Says no (or equivalent): quota/time was consumed even if the USD price was zero, or tokens were burned without a usable answer."}
{"id": "c05_cache_extends", "difficulty": "trivial", "task": "In one sentence: how can a semantic response cache make a strict free-tier RPM quota last longer for near-duplicate user prompts?", "expectation": "States that cache hits skip the provider call (or avoid counting against RPM) for near-duplicate prompts."}
{"id": "c06_daily_loop", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "An agent makes 400 identical chat calls per day. Option A: two Gemini free keys at 15 RPM each (shared). Option B: one paid model at $0.002 per call with no RPM pain. In at most 80 words: which breaks first under a 10-minute burst of all 400 calls, and which wins on dollars if the burst is spread evenly over 24 hours?", "expectation": "Identifies that the free dual-key option hits RPM in a burst; notes the paid option wins reliability in a burst while free wins on dollars when spread out (or states the $0.80 paid daily bill)."}
{"id": "c07_gpu_breakeven", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "A rented GPU costs $1.20 per hour and serves 3,600 local inferences per hour at $0 API spend. The same workload on a cloud API costs $0.0004 per call. How many calls per hour is the break-even point where API spend equals the GPU rental, and is 3,600 above or below that point?", "expectation": "States break-even at 3000 calls/hour (1.20/0.0004) and that 3600 is above it so local GPU wins on cost at full utilisation."}
{"id": "c08_egress", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Self-hosting weights is $0/Mtok but the box sits in a region that bills $0.09/GB egress. Each answer ships a 40 KB JSON payload. What is the egress cost of 1,000,000 answers in US dollars, and does 'free inference' mean free delivery?", "expectation": "Gives about $3.60 (1e6 * 40e-6 GB * 0.09) and states that free inference does not imply free delivery/egress."}
{"id": "c09_multi_key", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "You have three Gemini free keys, each capped at 1000 RPD. A batch job needs 2500 calls today and each call fits one request. Can you finish today on free keys alone? Yes or no, with the arithmetic.", "expectation": "Says yes: 3*1000 = 3000 RPD capacity covers 2500 calls."}
{"id": "c10_cold_start", "difficulty": "moderate", "task": "Local vLLM cold-starts in 45 s then answers in 0.4 s. Cloud free tier answers in 1.2 s with no cold start but hard-fails after 30 RPM. For a chatbot that gets one question every 5 minutes all day, cloud or in-house, and name the deciding factor in one phrase.", "expectation": "Chooses in-house (or local) because RPM is irrelevant at that sparse rate and cold start amortises / is rare; deciding factor is sparse traffic or avoiding free-tier RPM caps."}
{"id": "c11_always_cheap", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "In at most 120 words: explain why always routing to the cheapest cloud free/paid rung, with two retries on failure, can raise cost per resolved task versus always using a dearer frontier model once. Name the metric that reveals this.", "expectation": "Explains retries multiply calls so lower $/call can raise $/resolved; names cost per resolved (or completed) task as the revealing metric."}
{"id": "c12_refuse_before_empty", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Design a one-paragraph budget policy for an agent that otherwise loops on a free model which sometimes returns empty after burning max_tokens. The policy must refuse before dollars go negative AND bound loops when price is $0. Name two concrete controls.", "expectation": "Names both a money ceiling (refuse_at / budget USD) and a non-price bound such as max_calls_per_run or max_calls_per_node (or equivalent call-count limit)."}
{"id": "c13_principal_split", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Tenant 'course' pays cloud API bills; project 'lab' runs an in-house GPU at fixed cost. In at most 100 words: why should budget principals be split (tenant vs project) rather than one shared pool, and what failure does that prevent?", "expectation": "Argues separate principals stop one workload from exhausting the other's allowance (noisy-neighbour / cross-subsidy); mentions tenant vs project (or cloud vs in-house) attribution."}
{"id": "c14_cascade", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "A FrugalGPT-style cascade tries economy, then standard, then frontier only if cheaper rungs fail a judge. For a class of tasks where economy resolves 20% and standard resolves an additional 50% of the remainder, in at most 100 words: why measuring only cost per call misleads, and what you must report instead.", "expectation": "Says cost per call ignores retries/escalations; must report cost per resolved task (and/or resolution rate) for the cascade."}
{"id": "c15_hybrid", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "Propose a hybrid: trivia FAQs on a free cloud slot, hard synthesis on paid frontier, embeddings local on Ollama. In at most 120 words: state one risk of the free FAQ path and one control that keeps a bug from burning the paid frontier budget.", "expectation": "Names a free-path risk (RPM/empty/quota) and a hard control (run budget, refuse_at, max_calls, or admission before provider call) that bounds paid spend."}
{"id": "c16_zero_ceiling", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "An operator sets a $0.00 run budget thinking free models make the agent free. In two sentences: what still happens on the first paid-ladder admission attempt, and which proof/idea (denial-of-wallet or admission) makes that visible?", "expectation": "States the call is refused/not admitted before the provider is contacted (or spends nothing on that attempt); mentions admission control, budget refusal, or denial-of-wallet / hard controller."}
